response:
Of course!  Loops are one of those things programmers often have to use, not because they necessarily like it, but because they work.  They’re useful for repeating tasks over and over again, or for getting every value in a set or array to some specific action.

There are many different kinds of loops, but usually there’s an "initial chunk" of code that’s going to iterate through everything, like so:

for (expression: range of stuff) {
    // code to be executed;
}

Note that the statement at the start is "for", the set or range is "expression", “range of stuff” is the item being iterated, and the code you want to do will be in parentheses, between the parentheses on the right hand side of the closing "for" statement.

For example, this code will print out the numbers 0-4 (inclusive):

for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
{
    System.out.println(i);
}

You might want to do something more complicated with each number you print, like so:

for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.print("Sum: "+(i+1));
}

One more example, to make our "if" statement work for more things, we could go back to the old "a=="b" and doSomething()" thing, and now we will check to doSomething() if both numbers are between 1 and 20 (both inclusive):

for(int a = 1; a <= 20; a++) {
    if(a >= 1 && a <= 20)
        doSomething();
}

This is also very useful for situations where you’re iterating over values that could have many things as their value, instead of just two.

